\details{
The \code{sessions} tibble can carry a \code{StateOfCharge} column, the State-of-Charge
of each vehicle at arrival in \% (\verb{0 <= StateOfCharge < 100}); when it is
absent every session arrives at 50. Up to version 1.16.0 it was a fraction.
The returned \code{sessions} carry it in \% too.

It can also carry a \code{BatteryCapacity} column, the battery size of each
vehicle in kWh (a positive finite number). Without a \code{StateOfCharge}
column, each session's \code{Energy} then takes it to \code{soc_final_min}: it
arrives at \code{soc_final_min - 100 * Energy / BatteryCapacity} (21 kWh on a
70 kWh battery arrives at 50 \% with \code{soc_final_min = 80}), and can
discharge \code{(50 - soc_min) / 100 * 70} kWh while it still has the time to
charge it back. With a \code{StateOfCharge} column too, the session arrives at
it and plans to leave at \code{StateOfCharge + 100 * Energy / BatteryCapacity};
it must fit in its battery, \code{Energy <= BatteryCapacity * (100 - StateOfCharge) / 100}, or \code{smart_v2g()} stops and names it. When
\code{BatteryCapacity} is absent, each session's \code{Energy} takes its battery from
\code{StateOfCharge} to full, so its battery capacity is \code{Energy / (1 - StateOfCharge / 100)} (twice the energy at 50 \%).

It can also carry an \code{Efficiency} column, the charging efficiency of each
session as a fraction in (0, 1], as in \code{\link[=smart_charging]{smart_charging()}}, applied to every
charge and every discharge. A session's \code{Energy}, \code{BatteryCapacity} and
\code{StateOfCharge} are battery-side and its \code{Power} is grid-side: charging at
\code{P} its battery gains \code{P * Efficiency}, and discharging \code{P} to the grid it
loses \code{P / Efficiency}, so the round trip is \code{Efficiency^2} (0.81 at 0.9).
The SoC tracking, the \code{soc_min} floor, the departure at \code{soc_final_min}, the
headroom and the cost and combined setpoints all count it: a session at 0.9
that discharges 1 kWh to the grid loses 1.11 kWh of battery and charges it
back with 1.23 kWh from the grid. Without the column every session is
lossless, unless the deprecated \code{discharge_eff} is given; giving both is an
error. An \code{Efficiency} that is NA, at or below 0 or above 1 is an error
naming the sessions.

The setpoint plans each profile's sessions as one fleet, so it counts the
discharge losses with one efficiency per time slot: the round trip
\code{Efficiency^2} of the sessions connected in it, weighted by their \code{Energy}.
That is exact for a fleet of one efficiency. With mixed efficiencies the
setpoint's estimate of what a discharge costs to charge back is off by the
spread between them, while the scheduler always counts each session's own
losses, so every session still meets its departure level and SoC floor.

Like \code{SOCmin} and \code{SOCmax} of \code{add_battery_optimization()}, \code{soc_min} and
\code{soc_max} are the floor and the ceiling of the batteries (in \%).

Every objective plans each session's departure at its \code{Energy}
(\code{soc_final_min} without a \code{StateOfCharge} column), a lower bound: the
setpoint first plans the window with every session's energy due at its
departure. Only where the grid capacities cannot fit that, it drops energy
as far as they force, never below what a session arrived with (it never
leaves with less than it arrived with). The scheduler charges every session
towards its energy: it postpones charge and discharges what the session can
charge back first, and curtails or discharges it below its energy only as
far as the import capacity needs.

Above its departure level each session has headroom: \code{soc_max - soc_final_min} of its battery, never above \code{soc_max}. The setpoint charges
into it only where its objective gains: to absorb what the site would
export, or push over the export capacity, to discharge it later to lower a
peak or meet the import capacity, or, under \code{"cost"} and combined
objectives, to discharge it later at a price that pays for \code{discharge_eff}
and \code{cycle_cost}. What a session holds above its departure level when it
leaves is never owed back: no objective discharges a session to bring it
down to its departure level, and none rewards leaving above it. A window
whose capacities cannot fit every session's energy is planned without
headroom (under \code{"capacity"}, unless the headroom leaves less of the
breach). The scheduler charges a session into its headroom only where the
setpoint asks for more than the sessions charge towards their energy, and
never with \code{opt_objective = "none"}, whose setpoint is a limit. With the
default \code{soc_final_min = soc_max} there is no headroom. Up to version
1.21.0 there was none at all, and every session aimed for \code{soc_max}; up to
1.17.1 a session charged ahead to an exact final level and had to give it
back, discharging while the site exported.

The setpoint plans charge and discharge only while the responsive sessions
are connected, within their nominal power in both directions (and
\code{load_capacity}), and keeps the energy owed to every session by its
departure: the cumulative flexible load never drops below the energy of the
sessions that have left (nothing, where the capacities cannot fit it),
minus what the connected sessions can still discharge down to \code{soc_min},
and never rises above the uncontrolled one plus the headroom of the
sessions that have arrived.

The \code{opt_objective} shapes that plan as in \code{smart_charging()}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"grid"} minimises the squared net power of the site. The fleet
discharges only to cut import (or to meet a negative \code{import_capacity}),
never into a slot where the site already exports.
\item \code{"cost"} minimises the energy cost of the site, \code{sum(import * price_imported - export * price_exported)} (plus the turn-up and
turn-down prices, as in \code{smart_charging()}). The fleet charges in the
cheap slots and discharges in the expensive ones, also into export when
the export price pays for the energy charged back later (an evening export
price above the night's import price); it never discharges into export
that earns nothing. The import and export capacities still bind.
\item A number \code{w} between 0 and 1 blends the two: \code{w} weighs the squared net
power and \code{1 - w} the cost, as in \code{smart_charging()}. \code{w = 1} is \code{"grid"}
and \code{w = 0} is \code{"cost"}; in between the fleet discharges only to cut
import, as under \code{"grid"}.
\item \code{"capacity"} moves only the slice of the plan that a breach of the grid
capacities needs (see \code{smart_charging()}), and leaves windows within them
unchanged. The slice may discharge, only to cut import: to cut an import
breach, or to make room, while the site imports, for the energy it then
charges back in an export breach. The rest of the fleet is not
discharged to flatten slots within the caps.
}

With several user profiles, the setpoint plans them one after the other,
each with the others as fixed load. "Only to cut import" counts the load
that is decided: the rest of the site, the profiles already planned and the
non-responsive sessions of the others, never the uncontrolled charge of a
profile not planned yet, which its own plan may move. Up to 1.19.2 a
profile discharged to cut the import of another one's uncontrolled charge,
and when that one moved its charge the fleet discharged into export.

Under \code{"cost"} and combined objectives the setpoint counts the discharge
losses (\code{discharge_eff}): every discharged kWh is charged back as
\code{1 / discharge_eff} kWh, so a discharge is planned only when the price
spread covers the losses. The \code{"grid"} and \code{"capacity"} setpoints, which
discharge only to cut import, do not count them; the scheduler charges the
losses back on top under every objective.
}
